---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Project.astro";
import Heading from "@/components/ui/Heading.astro";
import Text from "@/components/ui/Text.astro";
import ProjectContnetWrapper from "@/components/ui/ProjectContnetWrapper.astro";
import SwiperSlider from "@/components/ui/SwiperSlider.astro";
import DraftNote from "@/components/ui/DraftNote.astro";
import { loadImages } from "@/utils/loadImages";

import logoImg from "@/assets/logo/alfalogo-min.png";

const title = "Alfa Bank - Alfa Travel";
const years = "2023-2025";

const imageModulesFirst = import.meta.glob<{ default: ImageMetadata }>(
  "/src/assets/alfa1/*.{jpeg,jpg,png,gif}"
);
const imageModulesSecond = import.meta.glob<{ default: ImageMetadata }>(
  "/src/assets/alfa2/*.{jpeg,jpg,png,gif}"
);
const imageModulesThird = import.meta.glob<{ default: ImageMetadata }>(
  "/src/assets/alfa3/*.{jpeg,jpg,png,gif}"
);

const sliderImagesFirst = await loadImages(imageModulesFirst);
const sliderImagesSecond = await loadImages(imageModulesSecond);
const sliderImagesThird = await loadImages(imageModulesThird);
---

<Layout {title} {years} logo={logoImg}>
  {/* ---------- 1. CONTEXT ---------- */}
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Text>
      Alfa-Bank is Russia's largest private bank, serving over 22 million
      customers. Alfa Travel is its in-house travel platform: flights, hotels,
      train tickets and package tours, booked and paid for inside the customer's
      existing banking relationship, with bank bonus points usable across the
      whole ecosystem.
    </Text>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      I joined as the travel department's first designer and stayed for two
      years as its only designer, working across desktop and the mobile webview
      inside the bank's app, on the bank's design system.
    </Text>

    {/* ---------- 2. ROLE & SCOPE ---------- */}
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold">My Role</Heading>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      Sole designer for the travel department, owning flights, hotels, train
      tickets and tours end to end — from research and flows to final UI and
      handoff, on both desktop and mobile web.
    </Text>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">Over two years I shipped:</Text>
    <ul
      class="mb-[1.562rem] list-disc pl-[20px] text-[1.062rem] leading-[1.6] marker:text-[#9a9a9a]"
    >
      <li>Self-service ticket refunds and exchanges, desktop and mobile</li>
      <li>The travel homepage redesign and its recommendation blocks</li>
      <li>The hotel booking flow, designed from scratch</li>
      <li>
        Flight search and booking, rebuilt on the bank's components
      </li>
      <li>Search across four verticals: flights, hotels, trains, tours</li>
      <li>
        The personal account where users manage trips and tickets, extended and
        rebuilt on components
      </li>
      <li>Ancillaries and the smaller fixes across the booking funnel</li>
      <li>A/B tests on key steps of the booking funnel</li>
    </ul>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      This case goes deep on two of them — refunds and exchanges, and the
      homepage redesign — because they show the constraints I had to design
      around.
    </Text>

    {/* ---------- 3. PLATFORM HISTORY ---------- */}
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold"
      >The Platform I Inherited</Heading
    >
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      Alfa Travel did not start as Alfa Bank's product. It was another travel
      company's white label, recoloured into the bank's style. As the product
      grew more popular the bank decided it needed something of its own, and
      that is the point I was hired at: to rebuild it on Alfa Bank's own
      components — a hard requirement — on top of what the partner exposed
      through their API.
    </Text>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      That move traded flexibility for ownership. We could finally build what
      the product needed rather than what the white label allowed, but every
      screen was now ours to draw and had to fit the bank's design system.
      Desktop barely existed when I arrived, and the mobile product was a
      webview inside the bank's app, not a native one — so I was building both
      in parallel, with the mobile side shaped by the webview it sat in.
    </Text>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <DraftNote
    image
    label="Before / after: the partner's white label screen next to yours. Figma has 'Туры / Старый дизайн' and the Webview section — that comparison proves the story in one image."
  />

  {/* ---------- 4. DEEP DIVE: REFUNDS & EXCHANGES ---------- */}
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading class="mt-[100px] mb-[10px] text-[28px]" level={2} weight="semibold"
      >Refunds & Exchanges</Heading
    >
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold">The Problem</Heading>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <DraftNote
    label="What happened before: the user had to contact support, how long it took, where the request came from (business, support load, complaints), and the scale of the problem."
  />
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold">Constraints</Heading>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <DraftNote
    label="What boxed the design in: fare rules, airline policies, the partner API, legal and compliance sign-off, the bank's design system. Which of these was the hardest to get through, and what it cost the design."
  />
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold"
      >Two Flows, Not One</Heading
    >
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <DraftNote
    label="Why voluntary and forced refunds were split into separate flows — the key structural decision here. What each one demands from the user (a reason, documents, proof), and what you considered before landing on the split."
  />
  <DraftNote
    image
    label="Full flow map, exported wide from Figma and shown full-bleed with horizontal scroll (same pattern as the decorative carousels on the current page). One image for the voluntary refund: 36 mobile frames with arrows and branches. This is the single most important image on the page — it shows an end-to-end journey at a glance."
  />
  <div class="mt-[40px] mb-[25px]">
    <SwiperSlider sliderImages={sliderImagesSecond} initialWidth="270px" />
  </div>
  <Text class="mb-[100px] max-w-[452px] m-auto text-center text-[15px] italic">
    Placeholder slider — to be replaced by 6–8 key refund screens: request,
    confirm, amount breakdown, in progress, completed.
  </Text>
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold"
      >Edge Cases & States</Heading
    >
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      A refund has far more failure states than happy paths. The flow covers a
      non-refundable fare, an expired refund window, a rejected request, a
      refund the airline cannot process, a partial refund on a multi-segment
      ticket, and the case where the user declines the amount they were offered.
    </Text>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <DraftNote
    label="Confirm this list matches reality, and explain the 'Отказ от вырученной суммы' case — I could not work out what it means from the screens alone. Also: which of these states caused the most argument in review?"
  />
  <DraftNote
    image
    label="A row of 3–4 close-ups of the hardest states side by side (non-refundable fare, rejected, refund impossible, expired window), each captioned with the rule behind it. This is the 'edge cases and validation states' evidence they ask for — it should read as a deliberate set, not leftovers."
  />
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold"
      >Exchange & Price Difference</Heading
    >
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <DraftNote
    label="How the exchange flow handles a new ticket that costs more or less than the original: the top-up payment, the acquiring step, what happens when the difference goes the other way. The Figma exchange section has 37 desktop frames, so there is plenty to show."
  />
  <DraftNote
    image
    label="The exchange flow map, same wide full-bleed treatment as the refund one, plus the top-up / payment screens."
  />
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold"
      >Decisions & Trade-offs</Heading
    >
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <DraftNote
    label="The most valuable section on the page, and it has to be honest. You said you are not fully happy with the result but it works, and that it took many iterations and sign-offs. Write that down: what you wanted, what the constraints forced, what you would do differently now. A designer who names a compromise reads as senior; one who presents everything as a clean win does not."
  />
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold">Outcome</Heading>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      Self-service refunds and exchanges with automated price difference
      calculation removed the need for support intervention and reduced
      support inquiries for ticket-related issues by <b>74.1%</b>.
    </Text>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <DraftNote
    label="Anything else measurable here: adoption, share of refunds handled without support, time to complete a refund, error rate. One number is thin for a section this size."
  />

  {/* ---------- 5. DEEP DIVE: HOMEPAGE ---------- */}
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading class="mt-[100px] mb-[10px] text-[28px]" level={2} weight="semibold"
      >Homepage Redesign</Heading
    >
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      The homepage redesign started with people, not screens. I had calls with
      the heads of the travel business to collect their wishes and expectations.
      I also talked to the designer of the bank's main homepage and stayed in
      touch with them at every stage. For references I collected screenshots of
      homepages from major services in Russia and worldwide, partly through
      Mobbin.
    </Text>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold"
      >Designing Inside a Fixed Layout</Heading
    >
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      The bento layout was not my choice. The bank's main homepage already used
      it, so travel had to follow for the ecosystem to stay consistent, and I
      agreed every step with the designers of the main site. The work was
      finding what could vary inside that grid: the business team kept asking
      for promo banners, so I prepared different grid layouts for different
      numbers of banners rather than one fixed arrangement.
    </Text>
    <Text class="mb-[25px]">
      The main idea was to make the homepage more alive with travel
      recommendations: "Popular Destinations," "Popular Hotels," and "Popular
      Tours." Later we added an integration with Alfa Afisha, a service with
      events across Russia. I showed intermediate versions to stakeholders all
      the time, and we ran design reviews with the design team.
    </Text>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <div class="mt-[40px] mb-[25px]">
    <SwiperSlider
      sliderImages={sliderImagesFirst}
      initialWidth="267px"
      video={true}
    />
  </div>
  <Text class="mb-[100px] max-w-[452px] m-auto text-center text-[15px] italic">
    Existing homepage slider — keep, but add high-res final desktop and mobile
    screens next to it.
  </Text>
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold">Explorations</Heading>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <DraftNote
    label="Figma has 'Главная_бенто1' and 'Главная_бенто2', 'Главная1/2/3', and a whole section called 'В работе / Идеи / Проверки'. Which of these were real alternatives, what you tried, and why each was dropped. 'Thought process / exploration' is on their checklist as a very good signal, and right now the site shows none of it."
  />
  <DraftNote
    image
    label="Two or three homepage directions side by side, captioned with what each one was trying to solve and why it lost. Not a moodboard — a comparison."
  />

  {/* ---------- 6. A/B TESTING ---------- */}
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading class="mt-[100px] mb-[10px] text-[28px]" level={2} weight="semibold"
      >A/B Testing</Heading
    >
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <DraftNote
    label="Two or three tests, each as: hypothesis, variant A vs variant B, the metric, the result in numbers, and what you shipped. Their letter names skipping the data-driven testing process as a typical reason portfolios fail this stage, so this section carries real weight. Screens of both variants would make it much stronger."
  />

  {/* ---------- 7. DESIGN SYSTEM ---------- */}
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading class="mt-[100px] mb-[10px] text-[28px]" level={2} weight="semibold"
      >Working Within the Bank's Design System</Heading
    >
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <DraftNote
    label="Where the bank's components ran out and what you built: the refund state cards, the amount breakdown, the flight picker in the exchange flow, the search history block. One concrete example of stretching or breaking the system, and the reasoning. Revolut warns directly that leaning on a design system without showing personal craft is a common pitfall — this section is the answer to that."
  />

  {/* ---------- 8. OTHER WORK ---------- */}
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading class="mt-[100px] mb-[10px] text-[28px]" level={2} weight="semibold"
      >Also Shipped</Heading
    >
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      Alongside the two projects above, I designed the hotel booking flow from
      scratch, kept improving the personal account where users manage their
      trips and tickets, and worked on search across all four verticals —
      flights, hotels, trains and tours — including complex multi-city routes,
      search history, and passenger validation.
    </Text>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <div class="mt-[40px] mb-[25px]">
    <SwiperSlider sliderImages={sliderImagesThird} initialWidth="270px" />
  </div>
  <Text class="mb-[100px] max-w-[452px] m-auto text-center text-[15px] italic">
    Placeholder slider — final screens across hotels, search and the personal
    account.
  </Text>

  {/* ---------- 9. MENTORING ---------- */}
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold">Mentoring</Heading>
    <Text class="mb-[50px]">
      When a middle designer joined the team, I onboarded them: distributed
      tasks and helped them understand the project and the design system.
    </Text>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
</Layout>
